    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <div class="section-heading">Where to Invest</div>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Farmers face significant uncertainty about the cost and availability of farm labor. State minimum wages are rising, and the availability of farm labor is becoming more precarious. Upward pressure on labor costs. The <span data-field="labor_cost_driver">{{ labor_cost_driver }}</span> is one of the most significant factors for farmers to invest in machinery to replace workers, producing more efficiently and reducing labor costs.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Agricultural sugar's response to rising labor costs has been to invest in mechanization. The slowdown in unauthorized immigration has spurred investment in mechanization, including mechanical pruners and harvesting equipment. These technologies have presented difficult technical challenges that require coordina-tion throughout the supply chain, from nurseries that develop dwarf trees to farmers planting orchards and designing for machines to consume and harvest. Commodities that are expanding in popularity, such as berries, are facing challenges in apple varieties, are mecha-nizing faster than commodities whose acreage is shrinking, such as apricots and peaches.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <div class="section-heading">Migration and Uncertainty</div>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">President Trump's first three years of migration policy making have added several major impacts. First, the threat of government shutdowns at home, and tariffs and the suspension of for-eign aid abroad, resulted in more miles of border wall construction. Second, a sharp reduction in the number of Central American asylum seekers.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Second, many migration policy changes were blocked by judges who issued injunctions that prevented their immediate implementation. Appeals were filed, and many of these injunctions, allowing policies to go into effect that range from transferring</p>
